   800 K.
3. (Method) A method of specifying and verifying a designed element,
   comprising: computing a register identity from an input set; generating
   a blueprint bearing designer identity in a plurality of embedded
   channels; verifying the identity by read-back and refusing issuance on
   failure; and regenerating the blueprint from the input set to obtain a
   byte-identical document verified by cryptographic hash equality.
4. (Method) A blind verification method comprising: computing expected
   device parameters from material-class inputs; fixing acceptance bands
   and hash-committing them in a dated record prior to fabrication;
   fabricating the device by a first party; measuring by a second,
   separated party; and scoring the measurements against the committed
   bands, wherein a measurement outside a band invalidates the class input
   from which its expectation was computed.
5. (System) A heat-rejecting structure whose outer layer is its sole heat
   outlet, carrying no coolant and no moving parts, and comprising the
   element of claim 2 as its sensing skin.

ABSTRACT: A laminate heat-rejecting element integrates a wide-bandgap
semiconductor skin, a gradient insulation layer, and a structural back,
carries a register identity computed deterministically from mass and density,
is documented by identity-gated blueprints that regenerate byte-identically,
and is accepted or rejected against acceptance bands hash-committed before
fabrication under a blind, party-separated verification protocol.
